<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>net.cumba</groupId>
        <artifactId>cumba-oss-corej-parent</artifactId>
        <version>${revision}</version>
        <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>cumba-oss-cdisc-define-conformance</artifactId>

    <name>Cumba OSS coreJ :: Define-XML Conformance</name>
    <url>https://github.com/cumba-oss/cumba-oss-corej</url>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <inceptionYear>2026</inceptionYear>
    <description>
        Define-XML conformance validator implementing the CDISC Define-XML
        v2.1 Conformance Rules and the PMDA Validation Rules v6.0 Define-XML
        sheet against the corej-cdisc-define object model. Java only — no
        Python parity.

        LIBRARY ONLY: this module ships no CLI. The user-facing entry point is
        `CdiscValidate -vx` in clients/corej-cdisc-cli. A minimal standalone
        picocli CLI (DefineConformanceCli) lived here from the module's
        creation until 2026-09-01, when it was deleted
        (PLAN-rules-module-consolidation D14): it never bound a LibraryProvider,
        so every `Requires: library` rule SKIPped in it, making it strictly
        weaker than the -vx path it duplicated. Deleting it also removed
        picocli from a lib/ module, matching corej-cdisc-core, which has never
        shipped a CLI.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <mockito.agent>-javaagent:${org.mockito:mockito-core:jar}</mockito.agent>
        <!--
            0.70, against a project default of 0.80.

            ⚠⚠ THE HONEST NUMBERS, all from CLEAN runs (a non-clean run's jacoco.exec is
            appended to, not replaced, so it merges coverage from earlier runs whose tests no
            longer exist here — that is how an earlier reading of this value came out flattering
            and wrong):

              before PLAN-rules-module-consolidation : 0.946  (1678 covered / 1774 lines)
              after                                  : 0.71

            That is a 24-point drop, and it is not a measurement artefact. Two causes, both
            structural:

            1. D12 moved this module's 22 corpus-driven test classes to corej-cdisc-rules,
               because they had to follow the corpus (leaving them needed a cross-module path
               escape; a dependency the other way is a reactor cycle). They carried most of the
               coverage of eval/, tree/, checks/, ct/ and library/ — the evaluation machinery.
            2. Phase 6 added ~200 lines of new main code (the package format, its loader and
               rule selection). DefineRulePackageTest and DefineConformanceSelectionTest cover
               the behaviour that matters, but not every line.

            ⭐ THE COVERAGE STILL EXISTS — it is measured somewhere else. Those 22 classes still
            exercise this module's code, from corej-cdisc-rules. JaCoCo's per-module check cannot
            see across a module boundary, which is why this module was added to coverage/main in
            the same change: the aggregate is now the instrument that can see it, and the number
            to look at.

            ⚑ 0.65, not 0.70, by owner decision 2026-09-01. A one-point margin over the
            measured 0.71 would have red the build on a single uncovered line and cost someone a
            cycle for no safety gained — the per-module check is no longer the instrument that
            protects this code, so tightening it here buys nuisance rather than confidence.

            ⛔ So do NOT read 0.65 as "this module is 65% tested". Read it as "0.71 is what this
            module's OWN suite reaches, and the per-module check is no longer the right
            instrument for it". If the aggregate ever stops covering it, this number becomes a
            lie and the tests need to come back.
        -->
        <jacoco.line.coverage>0.65</jacoco.line.coverage>

        <!--
            65, against a project default of 80. Owner decision 2026-09-02: the pitest
            coverage gate was failing here.

            SAME CAUSE AS THE JaCoCo NUMBER ABOVE, so read that comment first. D12 moved this
            module's 22 corpus-driven test classes to corej-cdisc-rules because they had to
            follow the corpus, and pitest — like JaCoCo's per-module check — cannot see across
            a module boundary. Those tests still exercise this module's code; they are simply
            measured somewhere pitest is not looking.

            ⛔ So 65 does NOT mean "35% of this module is unreachable by tests". It means the
            per-module mutation run is no longer the instrument that sees this code, and
            holding it to a project-wide default reddens CI for a structural reason rather
            than a quality one.

            ⚠ Mutation target is deliberately NOT changed — it stays at the parent's 55.
            Only the line-coverage half of the gate is affected by the move.

            ⚠ This is inert unless the gate is armed: the pitest-fail-on-error profile
            (-Dpitest.failOnError=true) is what promotes .target to the effective
            coverageThreshold, and it must be combined with -P Pitest to run at all.
            ⛔ Never pass -Dpitest.coverage.threshold on the command line — a CLI -D clobbers
            every module's override at once.
        -->
        <pitest.coverage.target>65</pitest.coverage.target>
    </properties>

